{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 4, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-122b-a10b", "content": "Raymond III was a prominent nobleman who ruled the County of Tripoli during the twelfth century. He held significant power within the Kingdom of Jerusalem and served as regent for several young monarchs. His life was marked by political maneuvering and military conflicts with surrounding Muslim forces. Raymond faced challenges from various factions within the Crusader states while trying to maintain stability in his own territory.\n\n*Early Life and Inheritance*\nRaymond was born into the powerful House of Tripoli and inherited his title at a very young age. His father Raymond II had been assassinated by the Assassins. This event left the young count vulnerable to internal plots and external threats. He spent much of his youth under the guardianship of his mother Hodierna or other nobles while he matured. The region faced constant pressure from Nur ad-Din and other Muslim leaders who sought to expand their territories. Raymond eventually took full control of his lands and began to assert his authority over the coastal cities and inland fortifications.\n\n*Regency and Political Influence*\nHe became regent for Baldwin IV after the death of King Amalric. This role placed him at the center of power within the kingdom. Raymond acted as a stabilizing force during the early years of the leper king's reign. He managed to navigate the complex politics of the court and balance the interests of various barons. His relationship with the king remained strong for several years until differences arose over policy and marriage alliances. Raymond supported the faction that favored a more cautious approach to military engagements with Saladin. He also attempted to secure alliances through marriage but faced opposition from rival families.\n\n*Later Years and Death*\nRelations between Raymond and the king deteriorated over time due to disagreements regarding the crown prince and foreign policy. Raymond allied with Saladin and even allowed Muslim troops to pass through his territory. This move caused a rift with other Crusader leaders who viewed it as treasonous. He died in 1187 just before the catastrophic Battle of Hattin. His death left the county without a strong leader at a critical moment. The subsequent fall of Tripoli to Saladin occurred shortly after his passing. Raymond remains a controversial figure in Crusader history due to his shifting alliances and political pragmatism. His legacy includes both the stabilization of Tripoli and the deep divisions he caused within the Kingdom of Jerusalem during a time of great peril.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.0022097188}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -1.549722e-06}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.0039576422}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.10036532}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.008867182}, {"token": " prominent", "logprob": -1.0330415}, {"token": " noble", "logprob": -0.038966745}, {"token": "man", "logprob": -0.07359186}, {"token": " who", "logprob": -0.13182843}, {"token": " ruled", "logprob": -0.59229225}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.11727481}, {"token": " County", "logprob": -0.000365801}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -4.768373e-07}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " during", "logprob": -0.0020470098}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.027731718}, {"token": " tw", "logprob": -0.020023342}, {"token": "elfth", "logprob": -1.1205736e-05}, {"token": " century", "logprob": -4.5299635e-06}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.06237748}, {"token": " He", "logprob": -0.29475853}, {"token": " held", "logprob": -0.75041115}, {"token": " significant", "logprob": -2.3298483}, {"token": " power", "logprob": -0.01642306}, {"token": " within", "logprob": -0.13500755}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -2.7418175e-06}, {"token": " Kingdom", "logprob": -1.43161}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -1.192093e-07}, {"token": " Jerusalem", "logprob": 